# Event Delegation in JavaScript

Imagine you have a list of 10 items, and you want to perform an action whenever someone clicks on any item.

Instead of adding a separate click event to every item, you can add just one event listener to their parent element.

This is called **Event Delegation**.

### Example

```html
<ul id="myList">
    <li>Home</li>
    <li>About</li>
    <li>Services</li>
    <li>Contact</li>
</ul>
```

```javascript
document.getElementById("myList").addEventListener("click", function(event) {

    if (event.target.tagName === "LI") {
        console.log("You clicked:", event.target.textContent);
    }

});
```

### How does it work?

1. We attach one event listener to the parent `<ul>`.
2. When an item is clicked, the event bubbles up to the parent.
3. `event.target` tells us which element was actually clicked.
4. We check whether the clicked element is an `<li>`.
5. If it is, we perform the action.

### Why is it useful?

- Reduces the number of event listeners.
- Makes code easier to manage.
- Works well with dynamically added elements.
- Useful for menus, lists, tables and shopping carts.

### Remember

Event delegation uses **event bubbling** to handle events on multiple child elements through a single parent event listener.

One listener, multiple elements, cleaner code.
